1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:12,627
The International Space Station, a permanent platform in space, a stepping stone to the solar system, a scientific research center.

2
00:00:12,628 --> 00:00:16,000
Now, Space Stations on Modern Marvels.

3
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:30,001
November 1998.

4
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:34,420
A Russian proton rocket blasts into space

5
00:00:34,421 --> 00:00:36,720
carrying a special Russian-made module.

6
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:41,400
Two weeks later, the shuttle Endeavour lifts off

7
00:00:41,401 --> 00:00:43,240
carrying an American module.

8
00:00:43,241 --> 00:00:52,240
December 6, 1998, the shuttle catches up to the Russian section in space and connects the two modules.

9
00:00:52,241 --> 00:00:57,240
The International Space Station becomes a reality.

10
00:00:57,241 --> 00:01:04,240
In the years to come, the space station will increase in size to more than a million pounds.

11
00:01:04,241 --> 00:01:10,240
This is the largest peacetime collaborative effort in history.

12
00:01:10,241 --> 00:01:19,240
16 countries including Japan, Russia, Brazil, Canada and the United States are united in a single goal.

13
00:01:19,241 --> 00:01:27,240
To build the first international space station and secure humankind's permanent presence in space.

14
00:01:27,241 --> 00:01:32,240
Each country is contributing technology and resources.

15
00:01:32,241 --> 00:01:40,460
But a majority of the hardware is coming from the United States and Russia, historically unlikely partners.

16
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:49,760
The birth of the International Space Station began with an announcement in 1984 by President Ronald Reagan.

17
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:55,980
Tonight, I am directing NASA to develop a permanently manned space station and to do it within a decade.

18
00:01:55,981 --> 00:02:04,980
Setbacks, budget cuts and redesigns delayed Reagan's dream of space station completion in 10 years.

19
00:02:04,981 --> 00:02:10,980
But today's redesigned and smaller ISS still has the same purpose.

20
00:02:10,981 --> 00:02:12,980
Research.

21
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:20,980
Six international research laboratories will make up the bulk of the station.

22
00:02:20,981 --> 00:02:27,980
Scientists from around the world will perform experiments in space to benefit people on Earth.

23
00:02:27,981 --> 00:02:34,980
The ISS promises advances in such fields as medicine and metallurgy.

24
00:02:34,981 --> 00:02:42,980
To support this research, the ISS will also be comprised of service, control and habitation modules

25
00:02:42,981 --> 00:02:47,980
containing life support, navigation, communication and propulsion systems.

26
00:02:47,981 --> 00:02:57,980
solar arrays giant trusses will stretch 360 by 290 feet to collect energy from the sun

27
00:02:57,981 --> 00:03:06,980
construction of the international space station will require over 46 separate launches of equipment and supplies

28
00:03:06,981 --> 00:03:16,980
orbiting the earth at 17,500 miles per hour the station will be assembled in the harsh environment of outer space

29
00:03:16,981 --> 00:03:17,681
space.

30
00:03:17,682 --> 00:03:22,980
The intent is to provide an absolutely world-class research facility that we can

31
00:03:22,981 --> 00:03:25,340
operate at least for 15 years.

32
00:03:25,341 --> 00:03:29,980
The space station has lofty goals and an even loftier price

33
00:03:29,981 --> 00:03:30,681
tag.

34
00:03:30,682 --> 00:03:36,980
The $40 billion projected cost has led critics to question the merit of the station.

35
00:03:36,981 --> 00:03:43,980
But money hasn't been the only trouble for the ISS program. Two near-fatal incidents

36
00:03:43,981 --> 00:03:48,980
aboard the Russian Mir space station during the preliminary stages of the cooperation

37
00:03:48,981 --> 00:03:50,980
threatened the program.

38
00:03:50,981 --> 00:03:55,980
He came in too fast. By the time he started breaking, it was too late,

39
00:03:55,981 --> 00:03:59,980
and he began shouting again, get to the Soyuz, get to the Soyuz, we have to evacuate.

40
00:03:59,981 --> 00:04:01,980
And then it hit.

41
00:04:01,981 --> 00:04:06,980
The story of the International Space Station is filled with dramatic highs and lows.

42
00:04:06,981 --> 00:04:10,980
Budget cuts, a new president, and tragedy.

43
00:04:10,981 --> 00:04:17,980
All could have left the space station on the scrap heap of history instead of above our heads.

44
00:04:17,981 --> 00:04:24,980
It's a story that begins centuries ago.

45
00:04:24,981 --> 00:04:32,980
Since the dawn of humankind, the question of what lay beyond the clouds has fueled imaginations.

46
00:04:32,981 --> 00:04:37,980
Gods? Monsters? Alien beings?

47
00:04:37,981 --> 00:04:43,380
People endeavored not only to peer above the clouds, but to conquer that space.

48
00:04:44,140 --> 00:04:52,260
The idea of staying in space, of living in space, is almost as old as the notion of being able to travel to space.

49
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:55,120
I mean, most people didn't want to just go up and down.

50
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:57,340
They wanted to stay there when they got there.

51
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:06,580
Futurists have long dreamt of a space platform, which could be used as a launch pad to travel to the moon or Mars.

52
00:05:07,980 --> 00:05:16,780
A research facility, an observatory, a colony for a hundred thousand people, or even a navigational guide for ships.

53
00:05:18,780 --> 00:05:24,320
Edward Edward Hale wrote in 1869 a short story called The Brick Moon.

54
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:35,040
And in it, he depicts a group of people who are launched into orbit essentially around the Earth on a brick satellite.

55
00:05:35,041 --> 00:05:41,040
Hale's brick moon was little more than a masonry capsule catapulted into orbit.

56
00:05:41,041 --> 00:05:45,040
Visible to the naked eye, ships used it to set course.

57
00:05:45,041 --> 00:05:50,040
People around the world could look into the sky and see the sphere.

58
00:05:50,041 --> 00:05:55,040
For the first time, the dream of living in space was set to paper.

59
00:05:55,041 --> 00:06:02,040
But just achieving space flight would take nearly a hundred years to accomplish.

60
00:06:02,041 --> 00:06:07,880
With the birth of modern rocketry in the early 1900s,

61
00:06:08,380 --> 00:06:11,820
the space station was one step closer to becoming a reality.

62
00:06:12,980 --> 00:06:15,400
Scientists from the U.S., Germany, and Russia

63
00:06:15,401 --> 00:06:20,600
wrote theoretically about rockets, space travel, and even living in space.

64
00:06:21,920 --> 00:06:26,380
One Austrian author wrote with almost prophetic accuracy.

65
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:29,860
Herman Nordung's book, published in 1929,

66
00:06:29,861 --> 00:06:31,000
The Problem of Space Travel,

67
00:06:31,001 --> 00:06:35,900
describes in great technical detail what is required to build a space station.

68
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:38,960
He described all of the features that it would have.

69
00:06:39,240 --> 00:06:44,140
It was going to be a large wheeled vehicle like we've seen repeatedly since that time

70
00:06:44,141 --> 00:06:46,500
that would be able to create artificial gravity.

71
00:06:46,501 --> 00:06:50,680
He had solar energy and solar arrays that would allow it to be powered

72
00:06:50,681 --> 00:06:55,500
without any other type of electrical system in place.

73
00:06:55,501 --> 00:07:01,860
and it was kind of the prototype of what we think of when we think of a space station.

74
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:07,780
By rotating the large craft, centrifugal forces created the sensation of gravity.

75
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:14,160
Nordung, like many scientists, believed gravity was essential for long-duration spaceflight.

76
00:07:15,420 --> 00:07:21,720
Although underappreciated in 1929, 23 years later, rocket pioneer Wernher von Braun

77
00:07:21,721 --> 00:07:23,940
borrowed upon Nordung's vision.

78
00:07:24,580 --> 00:07:29,220
By the early 1950s, von Braun was working for the American Space Program

79
00:07:29,221 --> 00:07:31,880
and envisioned a space station like this

80
00:07:31,881 --> 00:07:35,240
as the logical step after sending a person into orbit

81
00:07:35,241 --> 00:07:37,440
and before sending a person to the moon.

82
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:43,760
But von Braun's vision was still just a far-off dream,

83
00:07:44,300 --> 00:07:47,220
for scientists in the United States and the Soviet Union

84
00:07:47,221 --> 00:07:50,300
were laboring to make man-space flight a reality.

85
00:07:53,940 --> 00:07:58,880
Relations between the two superpowers grew frigid at the dawn of the Cold War.

86
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:06,200
Fortunately, their competition would be played out high above the battlefield rather than on it.

87
00:08:08,760 --> 00:08:12,460
The Great Space Race was just beginning to heat up.

88
00:08:14,260 --> 00:08:17,260
The Soviet Union claimed the first victory.

89
00:08:17,900 --> 00:08:25,980
Trepidation gripped the United States in 1957 when the Soviets successfully launched the first satellite, Sputnik, into orbit.

90
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:32,880
For the first time, stunned Americans realized that not only did its Cold War enemy have the nuclear bomb,

91
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:36,580
the Soviet Union also had the ability to deliver it.

92
00:08:37,260 --> 00:08:41,680
The same rocket that achieved orbit could be pointed at the United States.

93
00:08:41,681 --> 00:08:53,279
I remember growing up as a child and it was drilled into our head every day at school of the evil communists and the Russians who were the bad people and they were

94
00:08:53,280 --> 00:08:55,200
just waiting to destroy us.

95
00:08:55,320 --> 00:09:03,760
And we learned the old duck and cover routines of in case we heard an alarm go off, we ducked under our desks in case of a nuclear attack.

96
00:09:03,761 --> 00:09:08,800
Sundays, holidays, vacation time

97
00:09:08,801 --> 00:09:12,260
We must be ready every day, all the time

98
00:09:12,261 --> 00:09:15,640
To do the right thing if the atomic bomb explodes

99
00:09:15,641 --> 00:09:17,560
Duck and cover

100
00:09:17,561 --> 00:09:20,720
This family knows what to do

101
00:09:20,721 --> 00:09:23,860
At the height of America's paranoia

102
00:09:23,861 --> 00:09:28,520
The Soviet Union triumphed again on April 12, 1961

103
00:09:28,521 --> 00:09:31,500
By launching the first man into space

104
00:09:31,501 --> 00:09:32,960
Yuri Gagarin

105
00:09:32,961 --> 00:09:45,360
despite several successful US rocket launches embarrassing launch failures only worsened

106
00:09:45,361 --> 00:09:53,580
America's sinking public morale President Kennedy needed something impressive to boost spirits

107
00:09:53,581 --> 00:09:58,580
and prove America's superiority he looked to NASA for an answer

108
00:09:58,581 --> 00:10:02,940
Werner von Braun suggested a bonafide space station,

109
00:10:02,941 --> 00:10:07,320
different than the small, less capable capsules NASA was launching.

110
00:10:08,440 --> 00:10:10,740
But von Braun's plans never took flight.

111
00:10:11,500 --> 00:10:14,760
Kennedy instead opted for something a little more dramatic.

112
00:10:15,660 --> 00:10:19,720
I believe that this nation should commit itself to achieving the goal,

113
00:10:20,420 --> 00:10:26,100
before this decade is out, of landing a man on the moon and returning him safely to the Earth.

114
00:10:26,101 --> 00:10:31,980
To meet President Kennedy's commitment, a space station had to be postponed.

115
00:10:33,060 --> 00:10:36,420
Beating the Soviet Union to the moon became the only objective.

116
00:10:37,200 --> 00:10:43,460
It would take an astonishing amount of resources, technology, development, and luck.

117
00:10:44,420 --> 00:10:50,180
Although the Soviet space program was in many ways more successful than the American program,

118
00:10:50,181 --> 00:10:58,300
the United States claimed the grand prize by first landing a man on the moon in 1969.

119
00:11:03,340 --> 00:11:05,860
Kennedy's objective was met.

120
00:11:07,440 --> 00:11:10,660
And I think the space race was the giant relief valve.

121
00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:16,320
We were not competing during the Cold War for ground as much as we were competing for people's minds.

122
00:11:16,321 --> 00:11:21,480
Russia conceded the moon to the United States

123
00:11:21,481 --> 00:11:26,280
and put their efforts into accomplishing the dream of the first space station.

124
00:11:27,280 --> 00:11:29,080
The challenges were monumental.

125
00:11:30,300 --> 00:11:34,180
The platform in space wouldn't be won without tragedy.

126
00:11:36,980 --> 00:11:42,120
Because scientists did not know if solid food was digestible in space,

127
00:11:42,121 --> 00:11:47,600
Food for early space flights was pureed and put into toothpaste containers.

128
00:11:48,420 --> 00:11:51,120
Space stations will return on Modern Marvels.

129
00:11:53,280 --> 00:12:05,156
Soviet engineers in Moscow faced overwhelming obstacles in the late 1960s designing a spacecraft capable of sustaining life for extended periods of time, hundreds of

130
00:12:05,157 --> 00:12:06,740
miles above the Earth.

131
00:12:06,741 --> 00:12:15,740
Orbiting the earth once every 90 minutes, the craft had to withstand extreme temperature fluctuations of 500 degrees.

132
00:12:15,741 --> 00:12:29,741
The craft had to be self-contained and required more sophisticated life support systems, power generation, waste removal and oxygen purification than on any previous

133
00:12:31,179 --> 00:12:31,879
craft.

134
00:12:31,880 --> 00:12:32,580
craft.

135
00:12:32,581 --> 00:12:40,340
As engineers worked to overcome the technical obstacles, physicians questioned

136
00:12:40,341 --> 00:12:44,777
how lengthy space travel would affect the human body.

137
00:12:44,778 --> 00:12:47,540
Would cosmonauts suffer digestive

138
00:12:47,541 --> 00:12:52,633
problems, muscle loss, or even psychological effects?

139
00:12:52,634 --> 00:12:55,900
The Soviet Union planned to answer

140
00:12:55,901 --> 00:13:00,660
all of these questions.

141
00:13:00,661 --> 00:13:08,860
In the late 1960s, Russia intensified efforts to build the first space station.

142
00:13:08,861 --> 00:13:12,560
They immediately got the world's first space station up, Salyut 1, which was

143
00:13:12,561 --> 00:13:14,620
initially a great success.

144
00:13:14,621 --> 00:13:17,380
On April 19, 1971,

145
00:13:17,381 --> 00:13:22,020
a Russian rocket lifted off the launch pad in Bacchanor, Kazakhstan,

146
00:13:22,021 --> 00:13:25,140
carrying the first space station, Salyut 1.

147
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:30,320
The U.S., concentrating on the moon, didn't have a space station ready

148
00:13:30,321 --> 00:13:33,320
and conceded that milestone to the Russians.

149
00:13:34,620 --> 00:13:37,080
Manned by a three-cosmonaut crew,

150
00:13:37,740 --> 00:13:42,500
Salyut 1 looked nothing like the popular wheel space station concept.

151
00:13:43,160 --> 00:13:45,800
It didn't produce artificial gravity,

152
00:13:46,280 --> 00:13:48,180
and it didn't have a lot of workspace.

153
00:13:48,181 --> 00:13:55,180
But the cramped quarters about the size of a school bus were still larger than any previous

154
00:13:55,640 --> 00:13:56,694
space vehicle.

155
00:13:56,695 --> 00:14:02,640
The 23-day mission of experiments and observations went nearly perfectly. Then,

156
00:14:06,220 --> 00:14:06,920
tragedy.

157
00:14:06,921 --> 00:14:13,220
During the return to Earth, their Soyuz transport craft leaked life-giving oxygen

158
00:14:13,221 --> 00:14:17,060
oxygen into the vacuum of space. All three

159
00:14:17,061 --> 00:14:22,220
cosmonauts suffocated as they re-entered the Earth's atmosphere.

160
00:14:22,221 --> 00:14:27,000
Paramedics tried in vain to resuscitate the three.

161
00:14:27,001 --> 00:14:31,540
Russia mourned the loss of their heroes.

162
00:14:31,541 --> 00:14:37,980
The event underscored the hazards of spaceflight.

163
00:14:37,981 --> 00:14:42,900
Although it ended in tragedy, Salyut-1 space station marked the beginning of an

164
00:14:42,901 --> 00:14:49,900
impressive history of Russians living in space.

165
00:14:50,900 --> 00:14:56,900
But the Americans weren't far behind. Delayed by their goal of reaching the moon,

166
00:14:56,901 --> 00:15:02,900
they wouldn't have the first station, but they pledged to have the best.

167
00:15:02,901 --> 00:15:08,900
Beginning in 1966, NASA developed what was referred to as the Apollo Applications Program,

168
00:15:08,901 --> 00:15:14,500
which became Skylab. It took some of the technology and one of the upper stages

169
00:15:14,501 --> 00:15:21,340
from the Saturn V spacecraft, reconfigured it into a long cylindrical space station,

170
00:15:21,440 --> 00:15:24,360
essentially, a space lab, which we called Skylab.

171
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:30,360
The 118-foot-long American station was outfitted with living quarters,

172
00:15:30,860 --> 00:15:38,140
telescopes, and scientific experiments, making it far larger and more sophisticated than the Soviet station.

173
00:15:38,900 --> 00:15:43,160
8. We have ignition sequence as started.

174
00:15:43,161 --> 00:15:53,600
On May 14, 1973, two years after the Salyut launch, Skylab blasted unmanned into the sky atop a Saturn V rocket.

175
00:15:55,300 --> 00:15:59,540
It was a brilliant launch, but immediately problems developed.

176
00:16:01,660 --> 00:16:06,700
During the launch, Skylab lost a meteoroid shield that also acted to shade the station.

177
00:16:07,620 --> 00:16:15,200
At ground control, panicked engineers watched helplessly as temperatures quickly rose inside the station to dangerous levels.

178
00:16:17,040 --> 00:16:25,840
More devastating news came when ground controllers realized one of the solar arrays that provided power to the station had ripped off during the launch,

179
00:16:26,180 --> 00:16:28,280
and another array did not fully deploy.

180
00:16:28,281 --> 00:16:37,280
Without power, Skylab risked overheating or freezing, dooming the $2.5 billion station.

181
00:16:37,281 --> 00:16:44,280
NASA engineers worked day and night to find a solution.

182
00:16:44,281 --> 00:16:50,280
The first crew scheduled to be launched separately in rendezvous with the station was delayed

183
00:16:50,281 --> 00:16:55,280
and given a crash course in Skylab repair.

184
00:16:55,281 --> 00:17:03,280
After 10 days of intense training, astronauts Charles Conrad, Joseph Kerwin and Paul Weitz were launched into orbit

185
00:17:03,281 --> 00:17:09,280
and positioned their Apollo capsule to dock with the crippled station.

186
00:17:09,281 --> 00:17:17,280
Inside Skylab, the temperature soared to 130 degrees Fahrenheit.

187
00:17:17,281 --> 00:17:29,608
A series of dangerous spacewalks freed the stuck solar array, and a sunshade placed over the exterior of the station slowly cooled interior temperatures to livable

188
00:17:29,609 --> 00:17:30,440
conditions.

189
00:17:31,220 --> 00:17:33,340
The quick response saved the station.

190
00:17:34,980 --> 00:17:39,860
Triumphant, the first Skylab crew settled into their new weightless home.

191
00:17:40,240 --> 00:17:46,540
Now, the astronauts could do all that fun stuff the rest of Earth could only dream about.

192
00:17:46,541 --> 00:17:56,540
Besides providing a cosmic playhouse, the near weightless environment on Skylab offered the opportunity to study materials in a unique way.

193
00:17:56,541 --> 00:18:02,540
Gravity, we don't realize how it affects so many aspects of our life.

194
00:18:02,541 --> 00:18:10,540
There are certain medicines that are impossible to do down here on the Earth, but up in space, they're able to crystallize some of these medications

195
00:18:10,541 --> 00:18:16,460
and very much much stronger crystals much more perfect crystals and enable

196
00:18:16,461 --> 00:18:20,720
some things to be done that they can't do down here. During the three missions

197
00:18:20,721 --> 00:18:26,100
aboard Skylab astronauts also performed countless experiments on the effects of

198
00:18:26,101 --> 00:18:30,860
weightlessness on the body. For years scientists knew the low impact

199
00:18:30,861 --> 00:18:36,100
environment in space caused muscles to atrophy. The Americans used various

200
00:18:36,101 --> 00:18:40,840
exercise routines and equipment to counteract muscle shrinkage.

201
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:47,180
The Russians also developed exercise equipment for extended periods of weightlessness

202
00:18:47,181 --> 00:18:48,760
aboard a space station.

203
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:56,260
Engineers also created a highly advanced and extraordinary semi-closed environment

204
00:18:56,261 --> 00:18:57,500
aboard Skylab.

205
00:18:58,500 --> 00:19:01,480
Because of the limiting factors on a space station,

206
00:19:01,481 --> 00:19:07,680
finite resources such as air and water are valuable and preferably recyclable.

207
00:19:10,300 --> 00:19:15,360
Skylab's environmental control included a filtration and odor removal system

208
00:19:15,361 --> 00:19:20,020
that scrubbed the oxygen, removing carbon dioxide and odors.

209
00:19:21,120 --> 00:19:23,940
Cabin air circulated over beds of zeolite,

210
00:19:24,360 --> 00:19:28,540
naturally occurring silicate minerals which acted as molecular sieves,

211
00:19:28,541 --> 00:19:30,720
trapping CO2 molecules.

212
00:19:31,480 --> 00:19:36,720
The semi-closed system also included a sophisticated waste management process.

213
00:19:37,300 --> 00:19:42,940
Still today, after all these years, the number one question that astronauts are asked is how do they use the bathroom in space?

214
00:19:43,280 --> 00:19:48,620
I think it's probably best summarized by an old quote by Apollo 9 astronaut Rusty Swigert.

215
00:19:48,800 --> 00:19:50,440
He just said, there ain't no graceful way.

216
00:19:51,480 --> 00:19:56,380
In the absence of gravity, the station instead used a suction system.

217
00:19:57,080 --> 00:20:02,140
Skylab's state-of-the-art toilet came complete with seatbelt and footstraps.

218
00:20:02,860 --> 00:20:07,400
No one wanted to risk accidentally leaving the scene at a crucial moment.

219
00:20:10,280 --> 00:20:15,760
Bodily excretions were automatically collected, separated, and packaged for study back on Earth.

220
00:20:17,840 --> 00:20:25,960
On July 11, 1979, gravity finally pulled the unmanned station into the atmosphere.

221
00:20:26,380 --> 00:20:27,480
where it burned up.

222
00:20:29,620 --> 00:20:33,040
I think there was a general feeling that it was a real tragedy

223
00:20:33,041 --> 00:20:35,380
that we actually had to let it re-enter.

224
00:20:36,100 --> 00:20:39,340
Of course, it was defined as a finite program,

225
00:20:40,100 --> 00:20:43,100
but we had an opportunity at one point to re-boost it.

226
00:20:43,400 --> 00:20:44,440
It wasn't funded.

227
00:20:45,240 --> 00:20:47,620
The station performed admirably.

228
00:20:48,480 --> 00:20:50,740
Three crews served aboard Skyland.

229
00:20:50,741 --> 00:20:54,220
The last returned February 8, 1974,

230
00:20:54,221 --> 00:20:58,100
after a world record 84 days in space.

231
00:21:00,360 --> 00:21:05,420
Skylab's last crew temporarily beat the Russian duration record for living in space,

232
00:21:05,640 --> 00:21:07,100
but that wouldn't last long.

233
00:21:07,940 --> 00:21:10,740
As America worked on a reusable space shuttle,

234
00:21:11,300 --> 00:21:14,500
Russia intensified its space station program.

235
00:21:15,060 --> 00:21:19,420
The biggest and most impressive station was on the horizon.

236
00:21:20,420 --> 00:21:29,160
The space shuttle was initially intended to be a ferrying vehicle between the Earth and the space station to be built at a later date.

237
00:21:30,120 --> 00:21:33,320
Space stations will return on modern marvels.

238
00:21:34,260 --> 00:21:38,400
The early 1970s brought the birth of the space station.

239
00:21:38,960 --> 00:21:44,480
Both Russia and America launched stations, but they weren't equally committed to their programs.

240
00:21:45,480 --> 00:21:51,120
Skylab was a short-term project and abandoned after only three missions due to a lack of funding.

241
00:21:52,280 --> 00:21:58,380
The Soviets, on the other hand, launched six additional Salyut stations during the 1970s.

242
00:21:58,980 --> 00:22:01,200
They planned to stay in space.

243
00:22:02,080 --> 00:22:03,920
America was just visiting.

244
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:11,000
Even though we went up with a little spirit of competition with the Russians, it didn't last long.

245
00:22:11,001 --> 00:22:19,129
And the Russians just sit there and kept plugging along at it and just consistently working at learning what a space station is all about and how to build them and how

246
00:22:19,130 --> 00:22:20,200
to keep them up there.

247
00:22:20,840 --> 00:22:29,040
During the 1970s, the Soviet Union stepped up their space station program, continually improving their many stations.

248
00:22:29,920 --> 00:22:34,540
The U.S. planned to build an even more impressive station after Skylab.

249
00:22:35,240 --> 00:22:39,420
But first, NASA decided on an intermediate step, the space shuttle.

250
00:22:39,421 --> 00:22:40,121
One.

251
00:22:40,122 --> 00:22:46,980
And we have liftoff of the space shuttle Columbia on an international life science and microgravity mission.

252
00:22:49,180 --> 00:22:51,100
Houston now controlling the flight of Columbia.

253
00:22:52,260 --> 00:22:56,860
The shuttle provided a research environment similar to a space station.

254
00:22:58,440 --> 00:23:08,180
Aboard the shuttle, astronauts conducted experiments on plants, animals, insects, and even fire.

255
00:23:09,420 --> 00:23:16,420
Although shuttle flights provided exceptional research, the shuttle had its drawbacks.

256
00:23:16,421 --> 00:23:23,420
It was relatively cramped, and shuttle systems weren't designed for long durations in space.

257
00:23:23,421 --> 00:23:28,420
The shuttle is a wonderful vehicle, but it could stay in orbit only two weeks.

258
00:23:28,421 --> 00:23:33,420
It's kind of an extended camping trip, and no way is it a space station.

259
00:23:33,421 --> 00:23:38,300
Having the same basic needs as a space station, but with less room,

260
00:23:38,301 --> 00:23:45,620
the shuttle could only carry enough food, water, and fuel cell materials to support life approximately two weeks.

261
00:23:47,840 --> 00:23:52,800
But NASA made the shuttle into an all-purpose vehicle with great success.

262
00:23:53,900 --> 00:23:57,100
Still, NASA longed for another project.

263
00:23:58,220 --> 00:24:02,820
Then came the announcement on January 25, 1984.

264
00:24:03,420 --> 00:24:07,440
A long-awaited call resonating back to the early days of NASA

265
00:24:07,441 --> 00:24:13,460
when von Braun and other scientists dreamed of a space platform of superior capabilities.

266
00:24:14,140 --> 00:24:18,040
Tonight, I am directing NASA to develop a permanently manned space station

267
00:24:18,041 --> 00:24:20,000
and to do it within a decade.

268
00:24:22,860 --> 00:24:27,300
We want our friends to help us meet these challenges and share in their benefits.

269
00:24:27,980 --> 00:24:32,140
NASA will invite other countries to participate so we can strengthen peace,

270
00:24:32,141 --> 00:24:36,360
build prosperity and expand freedom for all who share our goals.

271
00:24:37,280 --> 00:24:39,920
Reminiscent of Kennedy's bold declaration,

272
00:24:40,500 --> 00:24:44,580
Reagan introduced plans for an international space station named Freedom.

273
00:24:45,440 --> 00:24:51,340
The collaborative effort also promised a far larger, more capable platform than ever before.

274
00:24:52,620 --> 00:24:55,980
But the Russians didn't care to participate.

275
00:24:56,840 --> 00:24:58,580
They had more immediate plans.

276
00:24:58,581 --> 00:25:06,380
In February 1986, the next generation of Russian space station obtained orbit, the Mir.

277
00:25:09,320 --> 00:25:13,940
Meaning peace in Russian, the Mir was like no previous station.

278
00:25:14,740 --> 00:25:15,840
It was expandable.

279
00:25:17,660 --> 00:25:25,740
Over the next several years, Russians launched additional modules to increase the size of the station to 120 tons.

280
00:25:25,741 --> 00:25:32,740
modules automatically snap together

281
00:25:34,020 --> 00:25:38,380
the Russians commuted between the ground and the station in the same tried and

282
00:25:38,381 --> 00:25:40,080
true Soyuz capsules

283
00:25:40,081 --> 00:25:46,080
that they'd been using for the past 15 years

284
00:25:46,081 --> 00:25:52,080
once docked the Soyuz vehicles also served as a scapecraft in case of an emergency

285
00:25:52,081 --> 00:25:59,080
Supplies were sent separately on unmanned Progress supply vessels.

286
00:25:59,081 --> 00:26:03,080
A tremendous amount of science has been conducted aboard Mir.

287
00:26:03,081 --> 00:26:09,080
It has given the Russians the absolute perfect example of what an orbiting space station should be.

288
00:26:09,081 --> 00:26:12,080
It was a very stable platform that orbited the Earth.

289
00:26:12,081 --> 00:26:15,080
They were able to send crews up and down very easily.

290
00:26:15,081 --> 00:26:18,080
They had crews that lived on board for more than a year at a time.

291
00:26:18,081 --> 00:26:19,069
a year at a time.

292
00:26:19,070 --> 00:26:23,260
The Russians have accumulated four to five times the amount of man-hours

293
00:26:23,261 --> 00:26:25,329
in space than the United States has.

294
00:26:25,330 --> 00:26:28,260
And it's simply because they've always maintained a

295
00:26:28,261 --> 00:26:32,400
man presence aboard that space station, which is phenomenal in itself.

296
00:26:32,401 --> 00:26:39,400
The Mir included several complex life support systems designed for the five to seven year

297
00:26:39,401 --> 00:26:43,960
lifespan of the space station. But most hardware was functionally simple.

298
00:26:43,961 --> 00:26:50,860
There's vast differences of how the Americans and Russians approach the same problem and challenge in space.

299
00:26:51,600 --> 00:26:54,660
The Russians have always designed things the simplest way they could.

300
00:26:55,140 --> 00:26:57,020
Then if it worked, they've never changed it.

301
00:26:57,021 --> 00:27:05,040
Whereas the Americans come along and they always want to push the envelope of technology, make things so sophisticated they supposedly can't break down.

302
00:27:05,400 --> 00:27:07,700
Then if it works, see if we can make it better.

303
00:27:08,420 --> 00:27:09,880
But things did break down.

304
00:27:09,881 --> 00:27:19,500
On January 28, 1986, the space shuttle Challenger exploded just after takeoff, killing seven astronauts.

305
00:27:20,600 --> 00:27:23,920
It was the worst U.S. space disaster in history.

306
00:27:24,880 --> 00:27:27,500
It also nearly doomed space station freedom.

307
00:27:29,120 --> 00:27:34,160
In addition, cost overruns and smaller-than-predicted budgets delayed the station.

308
00:27:34,161 --> 00:27:40,200
Reagan's commitment for a completed station within a decade could not be met.

309
00:27:41,380 --> 00:27:44,320
The fledgling ISS faced termination.

310
00:27:46,260 --> 00:27:54,400
Space research has provided such consumer products as cell phones, cordless power tools, and Mylar food packaging.

311
00:27:55,160 --> 00:27:58,260
Space stations will return on modern marvels.

312
00:28:00,680 --> 00:28:03,660
The 1990s brought world change.

313
00:28:04,160 --> 00:28:04,860
The U.S.

314
00:28:04,861 --> 00:28:11,260
started rebuilding relations with Russia and became involved in the largest military conflict since Vietnam.

315
00:28:12,340 --> 00:28:16,440
The greatest threat to NASA's space station, however, came from within.

316
00:28:17,380 --> 00:28:18,080
The U.S.

317
00:28:18,081 --> 00:28:25,540
had already spent $8 billion on the ISS, which threatened to climb to $31 billion before completion.

318
00:28:26,580 --> 00:28:29,860
Newly elected President Clinton mandated a leaner NASA.

319
00:28:30,680 --> 00:28:35,940
Reluctantly, NASA redesigned the station, creating this smaller, restricted version of freedom.

320
00:28:37,400 --> 00:28:43,500
Then came the most significant development in the history of U.S.-Russia space relations.

321
00:28:45,340 --> 00:28:51,040
President Clinton invited the Russians once again aboard the International Space Station.

322
00:28:51,660 --> 00:28:54,920
A group of advocates, both inside and outside the government,

323
00:28:54,921 --> 00:28:59,680
developed with the notion that Russia could bring lots of experience,

324
00:28:59,980 --> 00:29:03,980
lots of capability to the space station program,

325
00:29:04,140 --> 00:29:07,060
plus engaging Russia with the space station

326
00:29:07,061 --> 00:29:12,380
would provide something for very talented technical people in Russia to do

327
00:29:12,381 --> 00:29:17,320
rather than selling their services to the Iraqs or Libyas or North Koreas of the world.

328
00:29:17,321 --> 00:29:21,320
So it was a strategic choice ultimately by the Clinton administration,

329
00:29:21,321 --> 00:29:27,080
administration both for space program reasons and broader security reasons to invite Russia in.

330
00:29:27,780 --> 00:29:32,533
Russia gladly accepted the offer and the 400 million dollars the U.S.

331
00:29:32,534 --> 00:29:37,080
pledged to pay for the experience only the Russians could provide.

332
00:29:38,040 --> 00:29:45,940
The Shuttle Mir, or Phase 1, program would be the first step before actually building the International Space Station.

333
00:29:45,941 --> 00:29:53,940
Phase 1 included docking the shuttle and Mir and sending several American astronauts to live aboard the station.

334
00:29:53,941 --> 00:30:03,940
With Russia's entrance into the ISS program and the public's growing support, the pared-down price tag began to grow again.

335
00:30:03,941 --> 00:30:14,940
After more than a year of training in Russia, astronaut Norman Thaggard rode a Russian Soyuz rocket to become the first American aboard the Mir.

336
00:30:14,941 --> 00:30:21,940
It was the dawn of a new era of international cooperation in space.

337
00:30:21,941 --> 00:30:28,940
Norman Thaggard spent three months aboard the Mir doing research and learning about living in space.

338
00:30:28,941 --> 00:30:40,940
Then on June 29, 1995, the shuttle docked with the Mir for the first time to take Thaggard home and make history.

339
00:30:40,941 --> 00:30:45,920
For five days, both Mir and shuttle crews worked together.

340
00:30:47,180 --> 00:30:52,700
Fortunate astronauts who took part in the historic event didn't know what to expect.

341
00:30:53,800 --> 00:30:56,660
It was kind of like a lived-in apartment.

342
00:30:58,100 --> 00:31:04,460
And, of course, the Mir didn't have the capability to send hardware and cargo back down to the ground.

343
00:31:04,780 --> 00:31:10,720
So they'd been accumulating all of this interesting stuff for, you know, a decade.

344
00:31:10,940 --> 00:31:16,180
So they were experiments on experiments strapped by bungee cords to the walls.

345
00:31:16,660 --> 00:31:18,720
But at the same time, very roomy.

346
00:31:20,340 --> 00:31:23,680
Thaggart returned to Earth with an American endurance record.

347
00:31:25,020 --> 00:31:30,220
Following Thaggart's landmark adventure, astronaut Shannon Lucid captured headlines.

348
00:31:31,120 --> 00:31:35,680
After spending four months in space, her shuttle return flight was delayed.

349
00:31:35,681 --> 00:31:42,680
Lucid's tour aboard Mir stretched to six months, a new American endurance record.

350
00:31:42,681 --> 00:31:48,680
Taking it in stride, Lucid only hoped to be home in time for Christmas.

351
00:31:48,681 --> 00:31:51,680
Lucid got her wish.

352
00:31:51,681 --> 00:31:56,680
The President and the American public welcomed her back to Earth with open arms.

353
00:31:56,681 --> 00:32:00,680
And a bag of M&Ms, her favorite candy.

354
00:32:00,681 --> 00:32:07,680
Soon after returning to Earth, she said she couldn't wait to get back into space.

355
00:32:07,681 --> 00:32:13,680
The next two astronauts to live aboard Mir didn't have the same enjoyable experience.

356
00:32:13,681 --> 00:32:19,680
John Blaha and Jerry Lininger found the isolation of space,

357
00:32:19,681 --> 00:32:23,680
the ever-increasing system breakdowns,

358
00:32:23,681 --> 00:32:28,680
and personality conflicts between crewmates exceedingly distressing.

359
00:32:28,681 --> 00:32:35,680
There are large cultural differences between our country and theirs.

360
00:32:35,681 --> 00:32:41,440
The crews train together and live together as much as they could on the ground, but you

361
00:32:41,441 --> 00:32:48,680
don't erase an individual's 40 years of upbringing and a couple of months of working together.

362
00:32:48,681 --> 00:32:54,620
But the culture clash proved to be the least of their concerns when Jerry Lininger faced

363
00:32:54,621 --> 00:32:57,580
a life and death situation aboard Mir.

364
00:32:57,581 --> 00:33:07,863
The youngest cosmonaut, Hoshul Azukin, goes to add what they call a candle, which is a bad name for an oxygen canister that they use to add oxygen when there's extra

365
00:33:07,864 --> 00:33:08,860
people on board.

366
00:33:09,180 --> 00:33:19,844
For some reason that night, for a reason that to this moment, so far as I know, no one has ever explained, the candle, in fact, the entire apparatus, burst into flame

367
00:33:19,845 --> 00:33:24,020
like a blowtorch out right in front of Azukin who goes like this.

368
00:33:24,021 --> 00:33:28,620
and it's a blowtorch like 18 inches that steadily grows and grows and grows.

369
00:33:29,100 --> 00:33:32,360
And everyone starts yelling, Pajar, Pajar, which is Russian for fire.

370
00:33:33,000 --> 00:33:36,180
Larry Korzun, the commander at that time, shoots in,

371
00:33:36,600 --> 00:33:39,420
bodily extracts Luzuk and throws him out and says,

372
00:33:39,520 --> 00:33:42,700
you know, get out of the way, I'm going to try to put this out.

373
00:33:42,860 --> 00:33:44,960
He knows that if he doesn't put it out soon,

374
00:33:45,460 --> 00:33:47,240
it'll either reach the other side of the hole

375
00:33:47,241 --> 00:33:52,140
or it could fall to some part of the hole, burn a hole in the hole,

376
00:33:52,141 --> 00:33:54,720
and they lose all their oxygen supply like that.

377
00:33:55,240 --> 00:33:58,340
They manage to get a series of fire extinguishers out,

378
00:33:58,600 --> 00:33:59,600
hand them to Corzoon.

379
00:33:59,800 --> 00:34:00,840
One runs out.

380
00:34:00,920 --> 00:34:01,760
He throws it aside.

381
00:34:01,860 --> 00:34:03,660
He gets another, throws it aside.

382
00:34:03,860 --> 00:34:07,040
Finally, he decides to switch from shooting foam on it

383
00:34:07,041 --> 00:34:08,940
to shooting water onto it.

384
00:34:08,941 --> 00:34:12,280
And he notices that eventually the white shining area

385
00:34:12,281 --> 00:34:14,540
seems to be shrinking and shrinking and shrinking

386
00:34:14,541 --> 00:34:18,040
until somewhat anticlimactically it goes out.

387
00:34:18,320 --> 00:34:21,880
And everybody kind of goes, we're still alive, we're still alive.

388
00:34:22,140 --> 00:34:28,140
Already in orbit longer than anticipated, the Mir was getting a bad reputation.

389
00:34:28,141 --> 00:34:32,140
Word spread about other system problems including testy computers

390
00:34:32,141 --> 00:34:37,140
and coolant leaks that oozed large antifreeze globules into the cabin.

391
00:34:37,141 --> 00:34:42,140
Jerry Leninger returned to Earth after 132 days

392
00:34:42,141 --> 00:34:46,140
and urged NASA to take another look at the soundness of Phase I.

393
00:34:46,141 --> 00:34:51,140
But NASA, confident in the program and the integrity of the Mir,

394
00:34:51,141 --> 00:34:54,060
sent up the fourth astronaut, Mike Foll.

395
00:34:58,080 --> 00:35:01,540
Foll enjoyed working with his cosmonaut crewmates,

396
00:35:01,940 --> 00:35:04,780
Vasily Sableyev and Alexander Lasutkin.

397
00:35:05,780 --> 00:35:09,000
Both Russians had been aboard when the fire erupted.

398
00:35:09,001 --> 00:35:13,660
Now they spent most of their time making repairs to the aging station.

399
00:35:15,780 --> 00:35:19,380
Public concern grew over the minor problems on Mir.

400
00:35:19,381 --> 00:35:26,640
But another near-catastrophic event would shake NASA's faith in the Phase I program.

401
00:35:27,740 --> 00:35:31,580
On June 25, 1997, during a training exercise,

402
00:35:32,140 --> 00:35:40,140
the Russian commander Vasily Sobleev attempted to dock an unmanned Progress resupply vessel by remote control.

403
00:35:40,500 --> 00:35:46,520
They wanted him to do it by sight, to estimate, to guesstimate with a stopwatch and a laser rangefinder

404
00:35:46,521 --> 00:35:47,820
how fast it was coming.

405
00:35:47,821 --> 00:35:55,420
Well, the fact was, after all he'd been through, after a fire that they'd had, the near collision that they had, he was not in real good shape.

406
00:35:55,900 --> 00:36:05,500
They sent it in on what's called a very hot trajectory, very steep, very fast, and through a combination of human and systemic errors, he just couldn't handle it.

407
00:36:05,620 --> 00:36:06,661
It came in too fast.

408
00:36:06,662 --> 00:36:10,720
By the time he started breaking, it was too late, and he began shouting again,

409
00:36:11,020 --> 00:36:13,260
Get to the Soyuz, get to the Soyuz, we have to evacuate.

410
00:36:13,880 --> 00:36:14,580
And then it hit.

411
00:36:14,581 --> 00:36:24,620
When the progress hit the station, Mike Fole and Sasha Lazutkin both scrambled into action and quickly realized that they had a hole in the side of the station, a

412
00:36:24,621 --> 00:36:31,880
decompression, and that they had to isolate or seal off that area of the station so that they wouldn't lose the air.

413
00:36:32,440 --> 00:36:35,880
The vacuum of space was sucking the oxygen from the station.

414
00:36:36,480 --> 00:36:41,660
Fole and Lazutkin managed to disconnect cables running between modules and close the hatch.

415
00:36:41,661 --> 00:36:46,660
The crew's quick response saved their lives and the station.

416
00:36:48,660 --> 00:36:54,660
After the incident, many Americans demanded Foll be removed from the decaying station.

417
00:36:55,660 --> 00:37:02,660
We had gone through the exhaustive process that NASA goes through to identify the hazards and to deal with them one by one.

418
00:37:02,661 --> 00:37:06,319
We also asked Mike Foll, is this a safe thing?

419
00:37:06,320 --> 00:37:09,660
Should we be bringing Dave Wolf up or not?

420
00:37:09,661 --> 00:37:15,182
up or not. And we talked about specific criteria that needed to be met.

421
00:37:15,183 --> 00:37:16,660
They were met. Dave

422
00:37:16,661 --> 00:37:20,660
went up and had a very successful and productive time and Andy followed him.

423
00:37:20,661 --> 00:37:28,660
In 1997, astronaut Andy Thomas returned to Earth after more than 130 days in space, ending

424
00:37:28,661 --> 00:37:30,701
the phase one program.

425
00:37:30,702 --> 00:37:37,660
In total, seven astronauts stayed 907 days in space conducting experiments.

426
00:37:37,661 --> 00:37:43,660
We did put some crew members at risk, and that's part of space flight.

427
00:37:44,140 --> 00:37:47,100
We could have said it isn't safe enough and quit.

428
00:37:47,280 --> 00:37:48,200
We didn't do that.

429
00:37:48,280 --> 00:37:52,300
We showed confidence in our partner, and we learned a lot out of that.

430
00:37:52,301 --> 00:38:04,920
I think the Shuttle Mirror, the Phase I program, was on balance a very positive success for the International Space Program and for the International Space Station.

431
00:38:07,660 --> 00:38:11,520
Astronauts lived aboard Mir during the Phase 1 program.

432
00:38:12,200 --> 00:38:18,180
Despite the system glitches, the fire, and the collision, the program was dubbed a success.

433
00:38:19,180 --> 00:38:24,160
The U.S. obtained invaluable experience, not just living in space,

434
00:38:24,500 --> 00:38:32,540
but learning how to deal with unanticipated problems that will inevitably arise on the International Space Station.

435
00:38:32,541 --> 00:38:40,300
At the end of phase one, the Mir station had outlived its life expectancy by six years.

436
00:38:41,260 --> 00:38:48,620
NASA urged Russia to terminate Mir and concentrate their shrinking resources on the International Space Station.

437
00:38:49,020 --> 00:38:54,760
But Russia was reluctant to give up the last symbol of its once glorious space program.

438
00:38:55,480 --> 00:38:59,180
One of the outstanding characteristics of the Russian people is that they don't give up.

439
00:38:59,181 --> 00:39:02,340
They have tremendous personal courage.

440
00:39:03,040 --> 00:39:07,400
By the late 1990s, dwindling budgets seriously threatened Mir.

441
00:39:07,960 --> 00:39:12,620
For years, the station hosted foreign guests to raise money for operations.

442
00:39:13,520 --> 00:39:18,420
A Japanese journalist paid $12 million to stay nine days on Mir.

443
00:39:19,160 --> 00:39:23,080
Cosmonauts even resorted to doing commercials while on the station.

444
00:39:29,180 --> 00:39:42,580
In 1999, Russia finally announced plans to decommission the 13-year-old station.

445
00:39:43,220 --> 00:39:49,500
The Mir will meet the same fiery death as Skylab did when it re-enters the Earth's atmosphere.

446
00:39:50,460 --> 00:39:57,400
It was a successful program, but Russia's future in space would be the International Space Station.

447
00:39:57,401 --> 00:40:04,400
Russia began building the first module for the ISS in December of 1994.

448
00:40:04,401 --> 00:40:09,400
While that module was launched in November 1998,

449
00:40:09,401 --> 00:40:12,400
and the U.S. module launched two weeks later,

450
00:40:12,401 --> 00:40:20,400
46 separate launches are required to ferry modules and supplies to the growing station.

451
00:40:20,401 --> 00:40:24,400
The Russian KERS automatic docking system

452
00:40:24,401 --> 00:40:29,820
system and the shuttle robotic arm are used to attach additional modules.

453
00:40:30,820 --> 00:40:37,760
But the station requires a good deal of hands-on construction, connecting cables, tightening

454
00:40:37,761 --> 00:40:40,020
bolts and fastening lines.

455
00:40:40,900 --> 00:40:46,482
A record 75 spacewalks are scheduled, more than all previous U.S.

456
00:40:46,483 --> 00:40:48,200
and Russian missions

457
00:40:48,201 --> 00:40:49,160
combined.

458
00:40:49,161 --> 00:40:58,160
The station's appearance resembles the modular design of the mirror, with some improvements.

459
00:40:58,161 --> 00:41:02,160
In our design, we put the solar rays out at the end of the truss.

460
00:41:02,161 --> 00:41:05,160
The solar rays will basically track the sun.

461
00:41:05,161 --> 00:41:10,160
The station will fly around the Earth such that the bottom of the station always points at the Earth.

462
00:41:10,161 --> 00:41:17,160
the Earth and the solar rays will rotate on large alpha bearings that will allow them

463
00:41:17,560 --> 00:41:20,368
to track the sun continuously and generate more power.

464
00:41:20,369 --> 00:41:21,980
The inside of the space station

465
00:41:21,981 --> 00:41:28,980
from a cruise point of view is we have a lot more volume, bright airy spaces, good food

466
00:41:29,320 --> 00:41:35,487
preparation, sleeping facilities, the human factors are very good overall.

467
00:41:35,488 --> 00:41:36,320
Eventually

468
00:41:36,321 --> 00:41:43,220
Eventually the ISS will grow to four times the size of Mir and offer a lot more room.

469
00:41:43,221 --> 00:41:47,480
But more room won't be enough in the isolation of space.

470
00:41:47,481 --> 00:41:52,080
Training is essential and patience a necessity.

471
00:41:52,081 --> 00:41:56,600
When you first get on board a space station you're faced with a daunting prospect of spending

472
00:41:56,601 --> 00:41:59,200
four or five months there as I was.

473
00:41:59,201 --> 00:42:03,440
You have to make a big adjustment into your lifestyle to accept the environment that you're

474
00:42:03,441 --> 00:42:05,700
in, to accept the confinement.

475
00:42:05,701 --> 00:42:07,380
and that's very hard to do.

476
00:42:07,800 --> 00:42:10,120
You have to set up a place where you're living,

477
00:42:10,400 --> 00:42:11,960
and you set up some personal effects,

478
00:42:12,420 --> 00:42:14,780
and create your small habitation area for yourself,

479
00:42:15,280 --> 00:42:17,300
and you learn to, after a while,

480
00:42:17,600 --> 00:42:21,580
get your personal needs met by the limited resources around you,

481
00:42:22,040 --> 00:42:23,500
needs of recreation and so on,

482
00:42:23,680 --> 00:42:25,500
as well as intellectual stimulation.

483
00:42:27,320 --> 00:42:28,700
Once you're able to do that,

484
00:42:28,701 --> 00:42:32,480
the time you spend up there becomes very rewarding and very fulfilling.

485
00:42:32,481 --> 00:42:42,480
The International Space Station promises advances in the fields of medicine, chemistry, agriculture and astronomy.

486
00:42:42,481 --> 00:42:48,480
Our space program has been one of the chief incubators of new technology in this country.

487
00:42:48,481 --> 00:42:57,480
It's been estimated there are more than 80,000 products in our everyday way of life that owes its life in some way right back to the space program.

488
00:42:57,481 --> 00:43:03,480
The ISS may even help humankind understand more about itself.

489
00:43:03,481 --> 00:43:09,480
I would not for one moment tell you that we won't have substantial surprises before we're finished.

490
00:43:09,481 --> 00:43:15,480
And that's just part of the job. We're doing something no one has ever done before.

491
00:43:15,481 --> 00:43:19,480
Somewhere in our future we're going to go back to the moon, go to Mars.

492
00:43:19,481 --> 00:43:23,480
Let's start laying the technological foundation for that right now,

493
00:43:23,481 --> 00:43:26,480
because there's going to be two things again that are going to happen.

494
00:43:26,481 --> 00:43:32,360
You're going to have output of new knowledge, and then you're going to have spin-off of new technology.

495
00:43:33,060 --> 00:43:38,920
And any time you push that hard for a new goal, you're going to have some assets that come out of that.

496
00:43:39,640 --> 00:43:47,840
In 1869, Edward Everett Hale predicted the first space station would be used as a navigational aid.

497
00:43:48,500 --> 00:43:52,560
Finally, the ISS may be Hale's brick moon.

498
00:43:52,561 --> 00:43:58,100
because of its size it also has a tremendous amount of reflective surface

499
00:43:58,101 --> 00:44:03,340
and so when it goes over in the evening after sunset or in the morning before sunrise

500
00:44:03,341 --> 00:44:08,080
there is going to be this huge beacon in the sky that we're going to walk out and see

501
00:44:08,081 --> 00:44:14,120
go over our heads that we as earthlings not as Americans and not as Russians and not as

502
00:44:14,121 --> 00:44:18,280
Japanese but as earthlings we can look up and take pride in and say that's ours

503
00:44:46,040 --> 00:44:47,240
The History Channel

